/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092316 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092316 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092316 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092316 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Conditional Probability Using the Law of Total Probability" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092316 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186091927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092316 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f0ebe700-63af-57d4-ae8f-9e09dac5cf76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1045 "Extending the Law of Total Probability" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186093201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092453 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0c0d6ec4-01bc-5b0f-8b44-162a9c686020" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092559 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092559 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143530" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092559 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092559 #edb/ref 1071 "A math teacher splits her calculus class into three groups such that $35%$ of all the students are in Group A, $35%$ are in Group B, and $30%$ are in Group C. If $20%$ of the students in Group A obtained the top grade, $10%$ in Group B obtained the top grade, and the probability that a randomly selected student from any group obtained the top grade is $0.255,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected student from Group C obtained the top grade?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092559 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d262f09b-86ba-5f7c-9ab7-53a1fc7fd15f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092559 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092559 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $T$ be the event that a chosen student obtained the top grade, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the student is from Group A,\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the student is from Group B,\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the student is from Group С.\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student obtained the top grade, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(T\\cap A)+P(T\\cap B)+P(T\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|B)P(B)+P(T|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $35%$ of students are in Group A, $35%$ are in Group B, and $30%$ are in Group C, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.35,\\,P(B)=0.35,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of the students in Group A, and $10%$ in Group B obtained the top grade, we have\n\n$$\nP(T|A)=0.2,\\,P(T|B)=0.1\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected student from any group obtained the top grade is $0.255,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G)=0.255.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(T|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|B)P(B)+P(T|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.255 & =(0.2)(0.35)+(0.1)(0.35)+P(T|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.255 & =0.07+0.035+0.3P(T|C) \\\\ 0.3P(T|C) & =0.15 \\\\ P(T|C) & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected student from Group C obtained the top grade is $0.50.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093201 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093201 #edb/ref 1061 836329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093201 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "34e9e254-fd36-5512-88cc-6abf808e561b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093201 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093202 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093202 #edb/ref 1061 836331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093202 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "92a781b8-11f6-5279-b328-89be09bb0155" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093202 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093203 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093203 #edb/ref 1061 836334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093203 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a17de8a8-a153-5e7d-be7b-53093b1da395" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093203 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093204 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093204 #edb/ref 1061 836335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093204 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "80843a9c-ed90-5e4d-9462-e0eb6c40068c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093204 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093205 #edb/ref 1061 836338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093205 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b7b7a4f5-8ab3-527e-b01f-844c9aeadcbe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093205 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093206 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093206 #edb/ref 1061 836330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093206 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1477f9c6-f997-5b76-9d95-747c31eb50a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093206 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093475 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that three events, $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3,$ exist within a sample space $\\mathcal S$ such that \n\n- $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3$ are *mutually exclusive*, and\n\n- the events $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3$ cover the *entire* sample space.\n\nWhen these two conditions are satisfied, we say that $\\mathcal S$ is the **disjoint union** of  $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3,$ and we write \n \n\n$$\n\\mathcal S = B_1 \\sqcup B_2 \\sqcup B_3.\n$$\n\nA diagram of this situation is shown below. Notice that $B_1, B_2,$ and $B_3$ cover the entire diagram but do not overlap.\n\n![](images/f5/f5f9f04eb6affb9dca1f7d4e7d66d76c4d520cbf66db93db60639ce896fb19af.png)\n\n \n\nNow suppose that we wish to compute the probability of an event $A$ that lies within $\\mathcal S.$ Let's draw this event on our diagram and label its intersections with $B_1, B_2, B_3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ed/ed6568369a3af39cf1f6ecd5cb481de20d6c31bbce0fa1be54eb9ae0ab4e7f20.png)\n\n \n\nThe **law of total probability** can be deduced from this diagram. It states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A\\cap {B}_{1})+P(A\\cap {B}_{2})+P(A\\cap {B}_{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we will only consider a disjoint union of $\\mathcal S$ consisting of three events. However, it should be noted that the law of total probability can be extended to an arbitrary number of events, that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A\\cap {B}_{1})+P(A\\cap {B}_{2})+⋯ \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}P(A\\cap {B}_{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathcal S = B_1 \\sqcup B_2\\sqcup \\ldots$ is a disjoint union of $\\mathcal S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093475 #edb/ref 1065 6295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093475 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d4103a1f-00a1-55d2-8e99-f1b230f8b348" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093475 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093476 #edb/ref 1064 "The law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(A\\cap B_1) +  P(A\\cap B_2) +  P(A\\cap B_3),\n$$\n\nwhere $B_1\\sqcup B_2\\sqcup B_3$ is a disjoint union of the sample space $\\mathcal S.$\n\nOften, it's more helpful to express this law using conditional probabilities. \n\nFrom the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cap {B}_{1}) & =P(A|{B}_{1})P({B}_{1}) \\\\ P(A\\cap {B}_{2}) & =P(A|{B}_{2})P({B}_{2}) \\\\ P(A\\cap {B}_{3}) & =P(A|{B}_{3})P({B}_{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above form into the law of total probability, we get\n\n$$\nP(A) = P(A|B_1)P(B_1)+  P(A|B_2)P(B_2) +  P(A|B_3)P(B_3).\n$$\n\nLet's see an example of how to apply this." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093476 #edb/ref 1065 6296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093476 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "344e2752-b37e-517e-8057-b24f42e51566" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093476 #edb/ref 1067 "The Law of Total Probability in Terms of Conditional Probability" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093477 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that a particular garden is divided into three small plots. There are $10$ roses in the first plot, of which $5$ are red, $12$ roses in the second plot, of which $6$ are red, and $14$ roses in the third plot, of which $4$ are red. What is the probability that a randomly selected rose from the garden is red?\n\nLet $A$ be the event that a chosen rose is red, and \n\n- let $B_1$ be the event that the selected rose comes from the first plot,\n\n- let $B_2$ be the event that the selected rose comes from the second plot,\n\n- let $B_3$ be the event that the selected rose comes from the third plot.\n\nNotice that the disjoint union\n\n$$\nB_1\\sqcup B_2\\sqcup B_3\n$$\n\ncovers the entire sample space, and that there are a total of \n\n$$\n10+12+14=36\n$$\n\nroses in the garden. \n\n- To calculate $P(A \\cap B_1),$ we take the number of red roses in the first plot $(5)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(36).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(A \\cap B_1) = \\dfrac{5}{36}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(A \\cap B_2),$ we take the number of red roses in the second plot $(6)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(36).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(A \\cap B_2) = \\dfrac{6}{36}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(A \\cap B_3),$ we take the number of red roses in the third plot $(4)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(36).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(A \\cap B_3) = \\dfrac{4}{36}.\n$$\n\nSo, to calculate the probability that a randomly chosen rose is red, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A\\cap {B}_{1})+P(A\\cap {B}_{2})+P(A\\cap {B}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{36}+\\frac{6}{36}+\\frac{4}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNote the following: \n\n- You may have noticed that there is an easier way to solve this problem. That is, to sum the total number of red roses $(5+6+4 = 15)$ and divide by the total number of roses $(10+12+14 = 36).$ However, you're encouraged to work with the law of total probability as it's essential to get used to the concept.\n\n- A little later in this lesson, we'll work with a version of the law of total probability that isn't intuitive. Understanding the law of total probability in its simplest form will help when things get trickier.\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093477 #edb/ref 1065 6446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093477 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9b6238d0-0917-5d62-b878-311127fce7fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093477 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492215 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186492947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492215 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186492953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492215 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186492970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492215 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186492977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492215 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186492981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492215 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492215 #edb/ref 1045 "Extending the Law of Total Probability" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492215 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 1